// ==============================================================================
// Trace E: 30-Access Mapping-Sensitive Three-Block Cycle (0x00, 0x40, 0x80 x 10)
// Demonstrates an architectural scenario where the Direct-Mapped cache strictly
// outperforms the 2-Way Set-Associative cache due to set indexing differences.
//
// Address Breakdown:
//   Address      2-Way [5:4] (Tag [31:6])    Direct [6:4] (Tag [31:7])
//   0x00000000   Set 0 (Tag 0x0000000)       Set 0 (Tag 0x0000000)
//   0x00000040   Set 0 (Tag 0x0000001)       Set 4 (Tag 0x0000000)
//   0x00000080   Set 0 (Tag 0x0000002)       Set 0 (Tag 0x0000001)
//
// Architectural Explanation:
//   In 2-Way (4 sets): bits [5:4] are 2'b00 for all three blocks. All 3 compete for
//   the 2 available ways in Set 0. The LRU replacement policy continually evicts
//   the block that is accessed next, resulting in 100% conflict misses (0% hit rate).
//
//   In Direct-Mapped (8 sets): bit 6 is part of the set index (bits [6:4]).
//   0x00 maps to Set 0, 0x80 maps to Set 0, but 0x40 maps to Set 4!
//   Block 0x40 is isolated in Set 4 and is NEVER evicted by Set 0 conflicts.
//   After the initial cold miss, every access to 0x40 is a cache HIT.
//
// Expected Outcomes:
//   2-Way Set-Associative: 0 hits / 30 accesses (0.0% hit rate).
//   Direct-Mapped Baseline: 9 hits / 30 accesses (30.0% hit rate, 33.3% steady state).
// ==============================================================================
00000000
00000040
00000080
00000000
00000040
00000080
00000000
00000040
00000080
00000000
00000040
00000080
